}

\section{Heatmap appearance}{

\itemize{
\item \code{show_values = TRUE} labels cells; \code{value_digits = 2} controls rounding.
\item \code{flag_threshold = NULL} adds no flags. A non-negative value outlines
cells at or above that absolute value; \code{flag_color = "black"} sets the border.
\item \code{scale_limit = NULL} uses the observed range. A positive value fixes
symmetric color limits for comparing plots. It does not change saved values.
}
}

\section{Interpreting output}{

Bars are anchored at zero. Width corresponds to effect size on the
contrast's native scale. For \code{method = "residual"}, this is the
observed-minus-expected average difference between groups, in score units. For
\code{method = "refit"}, this is the subgroup parameter difference on the
fitted logit scale when linking support allows a comparable contrast.
Residual differences do not isolate differential functioning. Linked refit
intervals omit estimated-anchor uncertainty and cross-refit covariance.
}

\section{Read the figure with its source table}{

Summary labels follow \code{Level | Group1 | Group2}. Both summary bars and
contrast heatmaps use \strong{Group1 minus Group2}. A positive residual contrast
means higher mean residual scores in Group1; a positive refit contrast means
a larger facet location in Group1. With the usual difficulty/severity
orientation, the latter means harder for Group1. Check the model's facet
signs and rubric coding before interpreting either as favorable treatment.

\code{plot_data(p, "pairs")} retains the source rows, counts and analysis conditions.
For summaries, \code{PlotStatus} distinguishes displayed rows, finite contrasts
omitted by \code{top_n}, and unavailable contrasts. The last two do not mean zero
or no differential functioning. If all contrasts are unavailable, the
summary cannot draw points; inspect \code{x$dif_table} or a contrast heatmap.
Heatmap cells with missing values remain missing. An observed-minus-expected
cell mean can still be shown when its cell is below \code{min_obs}; inspect
\code{sparse}. The scaled-residual and pairwise-contrast routes withhold such values.

\code{N_Group1} and \code{N_Group2} count retained response rows. Residual results also
give distinct-Person counts, which need not be independent observations.
\code{plot_data(p, "cases")} preserves residual response matches and exclusion
reasons; it is \code{NULL} for refit results, which have no such response audit.
Refit source rows instead report \code{ScaleLinkStatus}, \code{ContrastComparable},
fixed-target flags and subgroup linking details. A finite point or an
adequate linking screen does not establish formal DFF inference. See
\link{mfrmr_linking_and_dff} for the different linking screens.
